            <div xml:id="d767">

                <pb facs="0724" n="716" sameAs="frus:frus1940v04/pg716"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.002/960: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in Japan</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1940-01-10T18:00:00-05:00">January 10, 1940—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>16. Reference Shanghai’s 15, January 6, 2 p.m., in regard to Japanese
                    interference, in the matter of an appointment, with the administration of the
                    Chinese Maritime Customs.</p>
                <p>If the approach made by the American Consul General at Shanghai proves not
                    effective the Department desires that, unless you perceive <pb facs="0725"
                        n="717" xml:id="pg717"/> objection, you approach the Japanese Foreign Office
                    along the lines of the second paragraph of the telegram under reference.</p>
                <p>Shanghai should continue to keep the Department and you informed of any
                    developments in the matter.</p>
                <p>Sent to Tokyo via Shanghai. Repeated to Peiping and Chungking.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
